The most efficient way to handle balanced parentheses in q is to track the "depth" of the nesting using a cumulative sum. By treating `(` as `1` and `)` as `-1`, any segment where the sum returns to `0` marks the end of a top-level balanced group.

```q
separate_paren_groups:{[x]
    / 1. Remove all spaces from the input string
    x := replace[` `;` `;x];

    / 2. Convert the string into a list of characters
    chars := flip x;

    / 3. Map characters to weights: '(' -> 1, ')' -> -1, others -> 0
    weights := (chars=`() ) - (chars=`)`);

    / 4. Calculate cumulative sum to find boundaries
    / cumsum returns a list where 0 indicates a balanced group has closed
    depths := cumsum weights;

    / 5. Find the indices where the depth is 0
    indices := where depths=0;

    / 6. Slice the original character list into groups based on these indices
    / We use the indices to determine the start and end of each group
    / 'prev' helps calculate the start of the current group from the end of the previous
    starts := 0, indices prev;

    / Use a loop or a functional approach to extract the substrings
    / In idiomatic q, we can use the indices to segment the list
    { (x;y) -> flip x[starts;y] } each (starts; indices)

    / Refined return: convert the character lists back into strings
    { (s;e) -> flip x[s;e] } each (starts; indices)
    }

